      "writtenOn": "2022-11-12T21:46:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I used `ectool thermalset` to try out different parameters and now think I am closer to a suitable change (including Scout; moved fizz and kalista to crrev.com/c/4024283).\n\nDiscussion: these thermal parameters have defaults in `fan.c`, overridden in `baseboard.c` (if applicable) or `board.c` (if applicable). The EC firmware relies on sensors on the board, which do not directly measure the CPU temperature. In testing, I have been able to drive a Teemo (fizz-cfm) to have its x86_pkg_temp at 90 C, unthrottled at 3 GHz, for over five minutes, while its board (TSR0) is only in the 50s (C). Since I want to avoid this case, this is why I have changed the `ec_thermal_config` `temp_fan_max` to the 50s.\n\n\nThe `ec_thermal_config` has four members: `temp_host` and `temp_host_release` to define high and halt temperatures, and `temp_fan_off` and `temp_fan_max` to control the fan.\n\nBetween `temp_fan_off` and `temp_fan_max`, the temperature is linearly converted to a 0-100 percent, which is then converted to a fan speed in rpm.  The fan tables and fan_percent_to_rpm function are meant to produce some hysteresis, without which the default fan.c implementation can produce oscillations: board temperature increases a degree, leading to more fan cooling; then board temperature drops a degree, leading to less fan cooling; repeat.\n\nIn testing with genesis (which originally did not have a fan_table), I did observe such oscillations; and they have been reported for other Chromeboxes (see [My chromebox \"breathes\"](https://groups.google.com/a/google.com/g/chromeos-discuss/c/2YPUYQ2QvLU/m/Bv1FXfICDAAJ)).",
      "parentUuid": "d7336669_8ad946dd",
      "revId": "3373318f6a3487843212dc7b5f3da8ce63942bf0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
